WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.240
Manny: YouTube is arguably the
biggest and most influential

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.920
social-media platform in existence.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.880
But gone are the days when YouTube's

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.800
primary export was funny cat videos.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.580
Today, YouTube is the primary platform

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.140
for just about every topic of video,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.030
from workout tutorials to
beauty-guru feuds and this.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.558
Carlos Maza: There's a
lot of cool stuff in here,

00:00:22.558 --> 00:00:24.540
but I am not an energy-policy expert,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.629
so I have questions.

00:00:26.629 --> 00:00:29.617
Steven Crowder: What were you
holding, gay Latino from Vox?

00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:31.180
Manny: That's Steven Crowder,

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.100
a popular conservative YouTuber

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.550
making a name for himself on the platform.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.300
That video was a rebuttal to this video

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.050
by Carlos Maza, a producer at Vox.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.820
Maza: Trump had tweeted a bulls--- claim

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.590
that the Green New Deal would somehow ban

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.470
cars, cows, and the military.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.087
How would you ban a cow?

00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:50.230
Manny: While both
commentators are taking jabs

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.470
at the other side of the
aisle, the difference

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.020
between their language
couldn't be any clearer.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.310
Crowder: This is what
Mr. Gay Vox wants to do.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.070
Mr. Lispy Queer from Vox.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.620
The gay Vox sprite is wrong.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.693
Now, he could be a tranny, your honor.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.170
Manny: In a viral tweet thread,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.110
Maza complained to YouTube
about this harassment,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.900
and it removed the videos
in question from the site,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.530
underlining an effort to make sure

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.310
they're hosting a safe and inclusive

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.260
platform for creators.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.560
I'm sorry, what?

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.070
YouTube actually did
the complete opposite.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.013
YouTube responded to Maza by saying

00:01:24.013 --> 00:01:27.250
that while it found
Crowder's language hurtful,

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.890
he did not violate its policies.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.870
The question here is why not?

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.853
Does YouTube have a
responsibility to regulate this?

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.120
According to YouTube's
own harassment policy,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.450
videos where Crowder
ridicules Maza for being gay

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.220
should be removed from
the site immediately.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.090
If you look at the language
of YouTube's policy,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.300
it says "hurtful" language
is a potential justification

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.695
for removing a video, and
YouTube literally used the word

00:01:54.695 --> 00:01:56.952
"hurtful" to describe Crowder's content

00:01:56.952 --> 00:01:58.927
in their first response to Maza.

00:01:58.927 --> 00:02:01.560
As the story continued to gain traction,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.880
YouTube issued a second response.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.410
The site decided to remove
ads from Crowder's channel,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.337
but it's a lousy
punishment for two reasons.

00:02:10.337 --> 00:02:13.810
First, YouTube already
limits monetization on videos

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.130
about hot-button issues that
aren't deemed "brand safe,"

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.440
even when those videos come
from legitimate news outlets.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.860
Secondly, Crowder doesn't just
make money from YouTube ads.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.200
Under his videos, he includes
a link to his online store,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.990
where you can buy shirts like this.

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:32.120
To make this punishment even weaker,

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.710
YouTube said it would
remonetize his channel

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.510
if he removed that link.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.500
What is the point of punishing Crowder

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.290
for making hurtful content

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.710
if that content is still
available to the public?

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.537
This entire situation calls into question

00:02:46.537 --> 00:02:48.880
YouTube's willingness to restrict accounts

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.570
that perform well for them.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.190
Steven Crowder has millions of subscribers

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.850
and banks millions of
views with his videos.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:56.920
Because of YouTube,

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.400
his influence is strong,
and his engagement is high.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.870
Punishing him would limit his influence,

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:06.163
which in turn limits
YouTube's reach as a platform.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.838
With great power comes
great responsibility.

00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:13.160
YouTube might have started as just a tool

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.450
for content creators, but the company acts

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.310
as much more than that today.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.640
It has an algorithm that
recommends and surfaces videos

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.930
that lots of people have already watched,

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.600
dictating what gets in front of eyeballs.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.770
It's already doing things that influence

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.040
what people do and don't watch,
which means it's behaving

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.780
much more like a media company

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.020
than a simple publishing platform.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.980
Nearly 500 hours of video are uploaded

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.620
to YouTube every minute, and we watch

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.260
1 billion hours of
YouTube content every day.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.180
So yeah, the power is there,

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.240
but where is the responsibility?

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.850
In a blog post, YouTube
gave another reason

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.340
why it wouldn't remove Crowder's videos.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.660
It says it's because
the point of the videos

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.040
wasn't to demean Carlos Maza.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.890
This is confusing and
allows for other users

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:03.880
to say hurtful comments
with no repercussions,

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.950
under the guise that
those hurtful comments

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.070
aren't the purpose of the video.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.640
YouTube says it's gonna
revisit its harassment policy,

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.940
but all signs point to Crowder's videos

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.971
remaining on the site.

00:04:15.971 --> 00:04:17.929
By refusing to restrict Crowder's

00:04:17.929 --> 00:04:19.989
homophobic and racist comments,

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:22.140
YouTube is inadvertently greenlighting

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.490
the use of this language in
other places on its platform.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:28.220
I haven't even mentioned the
fact that Crowder's followers

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.660
went on to harass Maza for days.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.300
This is not OK.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.361
For a company whose influence
is growing exponentially,

00:04:35.361 --> 00:04:38.053
they could be setting
a dangerous precedent.

